\viewkind4\uc1\pard\cf1\ul\b\f0\fs24\'b6 Verse 1\cf0\ulnone\b0 
\par 1. Teach me, my God and King,
\par In all things Thee to see;
\par And what I do in anything,
\par To do it as for Thee.
\par 
\par \cf1\ul\b\'b6 Verse 2\cf0\ulnone\b0 
\par 2. A man that looks on glass,
\par On it may stay his eye;
\par Or if he pleaseth, through it pass,
\par And then the heaven espy.
\par 
\par \cf1\ul\b\'b6 Verse 3\cf0\ulnone\b0 
\par 3. All may of Thee partake:
\par Nothing can be so mean,
\par Which with this tincture: For Thy sake,
\par Will not grow bright and clean.
\par 
\par \cf1\ul\b\'b6 Verse 4\cf0\ulnone\b0 
\par 4. A servant with this clause
\par Makes drudgery dinine;
\par Who sweeps the room, as for Thy laws,
\par Makes that and the action fine.
\par 
\par \cf1\ul\b\'b6 Verse 5\cf0\ulnone\b0 
\par 5. This is the famous stone 
\par That turneth all to gold:
\par For that which God doth touch and own,
\par Cannot for less be told.\f1 
\par \cf1\ul\b\fs16 
\par }

